.TH BANDWIDTH\-LOGGER\-RUN 1 "2026-08-04" "bandwidth-logger 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bandwidth\-logger\-run \- run one speed test and record it, without a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bandwidth\-logger\-run
.RB [ \-\-scheduled | \-\-now | \-\-status | \-\-export
.IR PATH ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bandwidth\-logger\-run
performs a single speed test and stores the result, with no graphical
interface. It is what the background timer runs, and it is also useful for
inspecting the application from a terminal.
.PP
It performs no scheduling of its own. Each invocation runs at most one test
and exits.
.PP
Ordinary use of Bandwidth Logger does not require this command. See
.BR bandwidth-logger (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-scheduled
Run one test recorded as a scheduled test. Before running, a long gap since
the previous attempt is noted as an interruption. This is the mode the
systemd user timer uses.
.TP
.B \-\-now
Run one test recorded as a manual test, and print the stored record.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the scheduler state, which engines are installed, and the most recent
record. Runs no test.
.TP
.BI \-\-export " PATH"
Export the full history to
.I PATH
as CSV and print the number of rows written. Runs no test and does not alter
the database.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable output instead of a summary.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-external\-ip
Include the external IP address column when exporting. Excluded by default.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Log at debug level.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The attempt was recorded. This includes a test that
.B failed \-
a failed test is a normal recorded outcome, so one bad test can never mark
the timer's unit failed and stop future runs.
.TP
.B 2
The application itself failed, above all a database that could not be
written. The history is incomplete and the problem needs attention.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bandwidth-logger (1)
